<h1>partial order</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
An order defined for some, but not necessarily all, pairs of items.  For instance, the <a href="set.html"><em>sets</em></a> {a, b} and {a, c, d} are <a href="subset.html"><em>subsets</em></a> of {a, b, c, d}, but neither is a subset of the other.  So  "subset of" is a partial order on sets.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Formal Definition:</strong> A partial order is a <a href="binaryRelation.html"><em>binary relation</em></a> that is <a href="reflexive.html"><em>reflexive</em></a>, <a href="transitive.html"><em>transitive</em></a>, and <a href="antisymmetric.html"><em>antisymmetric</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="totalorder.html"><em>total order</em></a>, <a href="poset.html"><em>poset</em></a>.
</p>

<p>Authors: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a>,<a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPJT">PJT</a></p>

